+### Parameters and Edge Cases
+*   **Density Function**: Must be accessible for evaluation. For constant density, it returns a scalar. For varying density, it returns a scalar field value [S1].
+*   **Quadratic Bounds**: Using quadratic bounds instead of linear bounds allows the ray to move away from the entry surface more quickly, improving performance [S1].
+*   **Varying Density Pitfall**: Performance gains depend heavily on the amount of empty space in the scene. If the volume is dense everywhere, the overhead of the BVH traversal may not be offset by the skipping benefits [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Depends on the BVH traversal cost and the number of samples inside the volume. For constant density, it is $O(\log N)$ for skipping plus $O(1)$ for analytic integration. For varying density, it is $O(\log N)$ for skipping plus $O(M)$ for ray marching, where $M$ is the number of steps inside the volume [G].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the BVH, where $N$ is the number of nodes [G].
-- **Measured Performance**: On a Clouds scene with constant density, the 'Ours, Impr Quad.' method achieves a 5.7x speedup over naive Sphere tracing (5.6ms vs 31.9ms), reducing average octaves from 1892.0 to 196.3 [S1]. On the same scene with varying density, it achieves a 1.8x speedup over naive Sphere tracing (37.9ms vs 66.5ms), reducing average octaves from 4087.4 to 1675.9 [S1].
-- **Strengths**: Significant speedup for constant density volumes; efficient empty-space skipping for varying density volumes [S1].
-- **Limitations**: Performance gains for varying density depend on the amount of empty space; requires accurate exit point calculation [S1].
-- **When to Use**: Ideal for semi-transparent volumes with constant density or sparse varying density volumes [S1].
-- **When Not to Use**: Not suitable for dense varying density volumes with little empty space, as the overhead of BVH traversal may not be justified [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Depends on the BVH traversal depth and the number of samples. For constant density, the number of samples is reduced to 2 (entry/exit) plus BVH nodes visited. For varying density, it is proportional to the volume thickness and sampling rate [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance**: On a Clouds scene with constant density, the method achieves a 5.7x speedup over naive sphere tracing (5.6ms vs 31.9ms), reducing average octaves from 1892.0 to 196.3 [S1]. For varying density, it achieves a 1.8x speedup (37.9ms vs 66.5ms), reducing average octaves from 4087.4 to 1675.9 [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Significant speedup for constant density semi-transparent volumes. Effective empty-space skipping for varying density volumes outside the object.
+*   **Limitations**: For varying density, the interior requires full numerical integration, limiting the potential speedup. Performance is sensitive to the amount of empty space [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in "Fast sphere tracing of procedural volumetric noise for very large and detailed scenes" [S1]. It builds on the concept of using BVHs for sphere tracing in opaque volumes and extends it to semi-transparent cases by incorporating analytic integration for constant density materials [S1]. The technique leverages quadratic bounds to improve convergence speed near the entry surface [S1].
+This method is described in the context of fast sphere tracing of procedural volumetric noise for large scenes [S1]. It extends opaque empty-space skipping techniques to handle transparency by leveraging analytic integration for constant density and hybrid traversal for varying density.
 
